# CHAROS build log

## 2026-09-05 — TC-Nest learning-machine refresh, compatibility phase

- Audited the live NixOS host before mutation.
- Confirmed the current 25.11 system configuration does not evaluate on the
  26.05 stable channel because upstream removed unmaintained `neofetch`.
- Replaced `neofetch` with maintained `fastfetch` in the isolated refresh
  branch. Live activation remains pending a clean 26.05 evaluation and build.
- Preserved the dirty live checkout; this work is isolated from its modified
  `vibedeck` submodule and two untracked backup scripts.
- Slimmed the base Python environment to the libraries used by lightweight
  host services. Large vision, speech, and model dependencies now belong in
  project-local labs, avoiding a projected 25.2 GiB 26.05 system closure.
- Moved the declared Node.js runtime from 22 to current LTS 24 for the learning
  role.
- The revised configuration evaluates cleanly on NixOS 26.05. The dry build
  fell from 8,007.05 MiB download / 25,182.34 MiB unpacked to 5,262.49 MiB /
  14,547.77 MiB. No system build or activation has occurred; storage
  preservation and safe reclaim remain prerequisites.
- Corrected the README's stale claim that family members still reside on the
  Nest. Their former accounts are now explicitly preservation state, while the
  machine's current role is Nathan's isolated learning workstation.
- Completed a read-only storage inventory and recorded the preservation and
  cleanup boundary in `docs/tc-nest-storage-inventory-2026-09-05.md`.
- Installed OpenAI's digest-verified standalone Codex CLI `0.153.4` for
  Nathan's account. A fresh interactive shell resolves
  `/home/nate/.local/bin/codex`; authenticated `codex debug models` lists
  `gpt-6-astra`. The system `0.92.0` binary remains underneath as rollback.
- Selected a non-destructive LUKS2-in-file preservation design for the existing
  exFAT Portable SSD and added `cryptsetup` to the declarative tool cabinet.
  Created the 128 GiB container with a newly generated Vaultwarden-held key,
  formatted it as ext4, backed up its LUKS header independently, and passed a
  clean close/reopen, `e2fsck`, and canary checksum recovery test.
- Preserved the high-value and dirty filesystem trees with numeric ownership,
  ACLs, xattrs, hardlinks, and symlinks. A second pass filled the remaining
  `/home/nate` and historical-account gaps while excluding only named
  rebuildable caches and separately exported live data.
- Produced application-consistent PostgreSQL, Vaultwarden, Healthchecks,
  Dashboard, and CouchDB backups. CouchDB and the LangGraph lab were paused
  only for bounded copy windows and verified running afterward.
- Committed and exported the stopped `wolfden-forge` container, then validated
  its compressed Docker archive structurally and by SHA-256. Captured machine
  reconstruction metadata. The final gate checksummed 788,354 files across
  955,472 entries and 32,068,343,137 bytes. Wolfden compared with zero
  differences, and all 15 dirty repositories retained matching HEADs and dirty
  entry counts. Four explicitly recorded live-churn files changed after the
  snapshot; none is in the intended cleanup set. No source cleanup has occurred.
- With explicit approval, removed only the preserved rebuildable caches and
  build outputs, vacuumed the journal to about 1 GiB, removed the archived
  stopped Wolfden container/image and dangling Docker images, and cleared
  unused build cache. Available root space rose from 9,180,418,048 to
  24,586,104,832 bytes; all six retained containers recovered healthy.
- Built NixOS `26.05.8954.a5cc6f2c37bf` successfully. A guarded test activation
  exposed two migration issues: obsolete Scout timers made activation return
  failure, and hot-switching the cross-release Wayland/DRM stack caused the
  internal display and network session to wedge. The timed recovery was
  followed by a normal reboot into the unchanged 25.11 boot generation; the
  preservation SSD remained locked throughout.
- Disabled the nine legacy Scout pipeline services and their timers on TC-Nest.
  Their definitions remain as migration provenance, while family runtime work
  belongs on Cove. The corrected generation will be boot-tested rather than
  hot-switched across graphics stacks.
- Rebuilt and booted corrected generation 63 normally. Verified live NixOS
  `26.05.8954.a5cc6f2c37bf`, Linux `6.18.49`, zero failed systemd units, no
  matching DRM/greetd errors, healthy Syncthing `2.1.3`, and all six retained
  containers running with configured health checks green. Nathan's standalone
  Codex remains `/home/nate/.local/bin/codex` at `0.153.4`; Node is `24.19.0`,
  Python `3.13.15`, Git `2.54.0`, Tailscale `1.98.10`, and Docker `29.7.2`.
  The preservation SSD remained unmounted and locked throughout both reboots.
- With explicit approval, deleted NixOS generations 1 through 61 while
  retaining generation 62 as the 25.11 rollback and generation 63 as current.
  Garbage collection removed 6,769 unreachable store paths and reported
  8.0 GiB freed. Available root space rose from 8,585,211,904 to
  21,806,080,000 bytes, `/nix/store` fell to 40 GiB, and the regenerated boot
  menu now contains exactly generations 62 and 63. Post-cleanup system and
  container health remained green.
